predef: "l2src/lmx_arena.h.lm1"
predef: "l2src/lmx_message.h.lm1"
predef: "l2src/lmx_thread.h.lm1"
predef: "l2src/lmx_schedule.h.lm1"
predef: "l2src/lmx_service.h.lm1"
predef: "l2src/lmx_manager.h.lm1"

# lmx_settle.h.lm1 - the settling of a finished direct child (spec 19.29.8; the last open
# item of the core, OPEN_NOW.md item 5).
#
# Spec 19.29.8, verbatim: "After final successful completion, reclaim the Message's own
# execution history by default"; "After final non-successful completion, transfer its logical
# arena to its direct parent's available lifecycle endpoint without copying.  The parent
# adopts the blocks/ranges and retains the failed root at its own end-turn or equivalent
# serialized owner-only lifecycle boundary"; "Each closing Message settles only its direct
# children: successful own histories can be reclaimed; failed arenas are adopted before its
# own failed arena moves upward"; "A stopped parent may still accept handoffs while settling
# its children; running=0 does not mean that its lifecycle endpoint has disappeared"; "in
# P -> C -> G, with C and G failed, G's blocks first join C, then C's blocks join P.  Native
# addresses stay unchanged and P retains the failure root.  There is no ancestor scan of
# grandchildren, global crash manager or graph clone."
#
# Nothing here is new machinery: the transfer without copying is lmx_arena_attach, reached
# through lmx_child_handoff, which already refuses while the child is not handoff-safe; the
# record leaves the schedule through lmx_manager_remove, whose own header names this act
# ("its storage released by the settle that reclaimed it"); the child leaves the parent's
# list of live direct children through lmx_thread_child_remove.  What was missing is the
# SETTLER itself -- the act that decides, per child, which of the two endings applies.
#
# The readiness of a child is read as ONE predicate over its two flags, from the settling
# side (lmx_message.h.lm1 says exactly why: "the decision 'not running and handoff-safe'
# reads the two in ONE predicate, and it is read from the settling side").
#
# An orphan (a record whose parent went away) is NOT this act's case: 19.29.8 gives it its
# own policy under R0 (lmx_orphan.h.lm1), and "an orphan is settled only by that policy,
# never by its supervising root's ordinary settle step".
define: LMX_SETTLE_OK 0
define: LMX_SETTLE_INVALID 1
# The child is closed, but something under it is still ALIVE, and nothing is freed while that is
# so (Mikhail, 19.09: "не освобождать ничего, что ещё живо").  Its own live children are given the
# stop request and the reap is POSTPONED to a later boundary -- the child stays where it is, in the
# parent's list, in the service and in the schedule, with its arena untouched.  Without this, the
# success path reclaims an arena that a live grandchild's own record still answers in.
#
# THE POSTPONEMENT IS A WAIT, AND A WAIT ENDS (19.09, evening): the child under it was ASKED to
# close, and the moment of that ask is in the parent's own node.  A child that reaches no boundary
# of its own within its own band's window is closed by the maintenance rule -- without a turn --
# so this deferral is never forever, and the parent is never retained for good
# (`RF NO_CLOSING_TURN` / `RF WINDOW_CLOSED`, tests\lmx_app_reroot_forbidden_selftest.lm1).
define: LMX_SETTLE_DEFERRED 2

prototype:
    # Not running AND handoff-safe -- the one predicate the settling side reads.  A child
    # that still runs is not this act's business: its stop is a write to `running`, and the
    # closing that follows is the family-closure step (spec 32), not this one.
    fn: lmx_settle_ready (@: LmxMsg message) int
    # Settle every settled direct child of `t`.  `parent_arena` is the parent's own arena --
    # a thread stores no arena of its own ("the arena is the context of the act, so the caller
    # supplies it") -- and is where a FAILED child's blocks go.  `service` and `manager` are
    # the parent's, so that a settled child stops being a route and stops being stepped.
    # A child that is not settled is left exactly as it is -- but the walk does ONE thing to it
    # before the gate is asked: a child that was ASKED to close and has reached no boundary of its
    # own within its own band's window is closed here, without a turn (lmx_stop_close; the
    # maintenance rule of 19.29.8).  Only the CLOSE, never the request: a settle passes working
    # children by, and stopping them is the cascade's business.  For a DIRECT child of the root
    # this walk is the only boundary there is (the root is never settled), which is why the rule
    # sits here and in the stop act both.
    fn: lmx_settle_children (@: LmxThread t; @: LmxArena parent_arena; @: LmxService service; @: LmxManager manager; ulong: now) int
end: prototype
